<<~ ahu #/head>>

! toml-ast ~ two layers, because reading and writing want different things

A carrier's meta fence gets read on every ingest and written only when something patches it. Those
two paths carry different costs, so they take different engines.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The invariants

# ''The READ path stays SYNCHRONOUS and small.'' A spec-compliant pure-JS parser handles inline
comments and TOML tables with no WASM init, so ingest never awaits and never pays a module boot.
# ''The WRITE path PRESERVES COMMENTS.'' Patching a key through a concrete syntax tree keeps an
operator's notes where they wrote them; a read-modify-serialize round would silently delete every
comment in the fence.
# ''The writer LOADS LAZILY and never enters the main bundle.'' It arrives on the first write call and
ships as its own chunk.
# ''An absent writer falls back to the RAW STRING rather than failing.'' Offline, an old browser, a
unit test — the patch degrades to a string operation instead of taking down the carrier that asked
for it.
# ''One code path, both runtimes.'' The reader runs pure JS everywhere; the writer resolves from disk
on a node and fetches as a chunk in a browser, and nothing above this line branches on which.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-flattening-contract>>

!! What a TW5 field can hold, and what that costs TOML

A tiddler field holds a string or a list of strings. TOML holds more, so the reader flattens on the
way in:

* ''a nested table becomes PREFIX-KEYED scalars'' — `uncertainty.foo` arrives as `uncertainty-foo`,
so a TW5 filter reaches it without knowing TOML;
* ''a number or a boolean coerces to its string'' — the field contract admits nothing else;
* ''an array stays a list of strings'', which maps onto the tags-style field TW5 already reads.

''The flattening runs one way.'' A reader that expected to recover the original nesting from the
prefix only guesses where a hyphen came from, so the meta fence on disk stays the source and the
fields stay its projection.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

